        {
          "id": "GENUS_PODICEPS",
          "name": "Podiceps",
          "rank": "GENUS",
          "description": "The large grebes — superbly adapted diving birds with lobed feet set so far back on the body that they are virtually unable to walk on land. Grebes are among the most accomplished divers of all freshwater birds, using their feet rather than wings to propel themselves underwater in pursuit of fish. The great crested grebe's elaborate courtship display — the most complex of any Swedish waterbird — involves both partners rising chest-to-chest from the water in the 'penguin dance', shaking their spectacular rufous-and-black crests and exchanging gifts of waterweed. This ceremony was first scientifically described by Julian Huxley in 1914 and remains a textbook example of ritualized courtship behaviour. The grebes' beautiful plumage made them targets for the 19th-century feather trade; their recovery catalysed early British bird protection legislation.",
          "lineage": "Large Grebes",
          "children": [
